.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-archive-set-edit 1  "tapectl-archive-set-edit " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-archive\-set\-edit \- Edit an existing archive set (flags left out are left unchanged)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl archive\-set edit\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-min\-copies\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-required\-locations\fR] [\fB\-\-encrypt\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-compression\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-checksum\-mode\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-slice\-size\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-\-verify\-interval\-days\fR] [\fB\-\-warehouse\-copies\fR] [\fB\-\-preserve\-xattrs\fR] [\fB\-\-preserve\-acls\fR] [\fB\-\-preserve\-fsa\fR] [\fB\-\-dirty\-on\-metadata\-change\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-spanning\fR] [\fB\-\-copies\-at\fR] [\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-\-description\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fINAME\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Edit an existing archive set (flags left out are left unchanged)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-min\-copies\fR \fI<N>\fR
Minimum copy count
.TP
\fB\-\-required\-locations\fR \fI<LIST>\fR
Required locations, comma\-separated

Each must be a registered location (`tapectl location add`).
.TP
\fB\-\-encrypt\fR \fI<BOOL>\fR
Encryption enabled (true/false)
.TP
\fB\-\-compression\fR \fI<MODE>\fR
Compression mode
.TP
\fB\-\-checksum\-mode\fR \fI<MODE>\fR
Checksum mode
.TP
\fB\-\-slice\-size\fR \fI<SIZE>\fR
Slice size, e.g. "1G" (the default)
.TP
\fB\-\-verify\-interval\-days\fR \fI<DAYS>\fR
Verify interval in days
.TP
\fB\-\-warehouse\-copies\fR \fI<N>\fR
Warehouse copies expected (ADR\-0006)

Never set means "defer to the system default".
.TP
\fB\-\-preserve\-xattrs\fR \fI<BOOL>\fR
Keep extended attributes (true/false)

And the POSIX ACLs stored as them; false drops them all.
.TP
\fB\-\-preserve\-acls\fR \fI<BOOL>\fR
Keep POSIX ACLs (true/false; with \-\-preserve\-xattrs)

No effect of its own: ACLs are kept or dropped with the extended attributes they are stored as.
.TP
\fB\-\-preserve\-fsa\fR \fI<BOOL>\fR
Keep filesystem attributes, chattr flags (true/false)
.TP
\fB\-\-dirty\-on\-metadata\-change\fR \fI<BOOL>\fR
A metadata change makes a unit dirty (true/false)

Not read yet: dirty detection compares path, size and mtime only.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-spanning\fR \fI<BOOL>\fR
Units may span tapes by their slices (true/false)

From tapectl 1.7.0. Never set means "defer to [defaults]", which is off.
.TP
\fB\-\-copies\-at\fR \fI<NAME=N>\fR
Copies at a location, NAME=N (repeatable)

E.g. \-\-copies\-at home=1 \-\-copies\-at offsite=1; each NAME must be a registered location. Replaces the set\*(Aqs whole map. A unit\*(Aqs dotfile [policy.copies_at] replaces it in turn; never set means "defer to [defaults]".
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-description\fR \fI<DESCRIPTION>\fR
Description
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fINAME\fR>
Archive set name
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
